/* ===========================================================================
 * Crimson Slate -- a dark theme for Noggit Crimson
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * Part of Noggit Crimson, licensed under the GNU General Public License v3.
 *
 * Drop-in theme: the settings panel lists any directory under themes/ that
 * contains a theme.qss. Every url() below is written as
 *   themes/CrimsonSlate/images/<file>.png
 * and must stay that way -- the loader rewrites that exact prefix so the paths
 * resolve against the executable directory rather than the working directory.
 * Do not make them absolute and do not drop the "themes/" segment.
 *
 * The 34 PNGs in images/ are generated, not drawn. Edit the colour constants at
 * the top of generate_icons.py (the same tokens as below), then run
 *   python generate_icons.py images
 * from this directory. Stdlib only -- no Pillow, no ImageMagick, no network.
 *
 * check_qss.py in this directory is a static checker for this file. Qt silently
 * ignores anything it does not understand, so run it after every edit:
 *   python check_qss.py theme.qss .
 *
 * ===========================================================================
 * PALETTE. Change these values and nothing else to retheme.
 * ===========================================================================
 * These are the same values the application QPalette is built from in
 * ApplicationEntry.cpp. If you change one here, change it there too, or an
 * unstyled widget will not match a styled one.
 *
 * SURFACES -- the app gets LIGHTER as it comes towards you. Chrome recedes,
 * controls sit forward. This is the opposite of the previous revision, which
 * sank inputs into near-black wells; that model made every panel read as a
 * grid of holes.
 *   bg.sunken     #16181D   3D viewport surround, dock gutters, splitters,
 *                           menu bar, status bar, scroll troughs
 *   bg.base       #1B1E24   panel and dialog background, dock title bars
 *   bg.raised     #22262E   inputs, buttons, list rows, item views, the tool
 *                           strip, header sections
 *   bg.overlay    #2A2F38   hover, menus, tool tips, combo popups, the
 *                           floating bars drawn over the render
 *
 * STROKES
 *   stroke        #343A45   visible borders: inputs, buttons, popup edges
 *   stroke.soft   #262B33   hairline dividers: section rules, panel seams
 *
 * TEXT (contrast measured against bg.base #1B1E24, see check_qss.py output)
 *   text.hi       #EDF0F4   headings, active values, the focused control
 *   text          #C6CCD6   body -- default label and control text
 *   text.dim      #8A93A0   section titles, dock titles, captions, units
 *   text.off      #59606B   disabled; deliberately sub-threshold, that IS the
 *                           disabled signal. Sub-threshold for TEXT on a panel
 *                           is not sub-threshold for a 1px STROKE on a
 *                           floating bar -- it measures 2.12:1 there -- so it
 *                           does a second job as the edge of the HOVER plate on
 *                           the view-toggle bar. Different role, different
 *                           threshold; it is still never used as body text.
 *
 * ACCENT -- warm red. Selection, focus, checked state, the active tool. It is
 * the only saturated colour in the chrome; if it shows up anywhere that is not
 * "this is the thing you are acting on", that is a bug.
 *   accent        #E8543F
 *   accent.dim    #B33F2E   pressed
 *
 * Accent carries two DIFFERENT marks and the distinction is the whole of the
 * view-toggle bar's design, so it is stated here rather than only there.
 *
 * As a FILL it means ONE checked thing out of a set: the exclusive left tool
 * strip, a selected tab, a selected row -- "the thing you are acting on".
 *
 * As a 2px RULE along one edge it means a LATCHED TOGGLE is on. That is the
 * mark the tab bar already uses (border-bottom) and the tool strip already
 * uses (border-left), and it is what the view bar gets. The bar holds 20
 * independent visibility switches (counted from the live add_tool_icon calls
 * in ViewToolbar.cpp's second constructor; the 21st button on it is the
 * history QPushButton, which is not checkable), of which 8 are ON at startup
 * -- read off the BoolToggleProperty initialisers in MapView.h, not guessed.
 * Filling 8 of 20 faces with accent would be noise; ruling 8 of 20 bottom
 * edges is not, and it is the only mark in this palette that clears the 3:1
 * floor for a graphical state indicator on a bg.overlay bar (3.69:1, where
 * the best available NEUTRAL is 2.12:1). See the floating-bar block near the
 * end of this file for the per-state numbers.
 *
 * STATUS -- meaning, never decoration.
 *   ok            #4FB477
 *   warn          #E0A33E
 *   bad           #E0574B
 *   bad.wash      #2A1B1B   destructive button at rest, fill
 *   bad.edge      #5E2C29   destructive button at rest, border
 *   bad.press     #B8443A   destructive button pressed
 *
 * DERIVED. Three values the token table does not name but the sheet needs.
 * They are computed from the tokens above, not invented alongside them.
 *   stroke.hi     #454C59   hover outline; halfway stroke -> text.off. It has a
 *                           second job as the HOVER FILL on the floating bars,
 *                           whose own surface is bg.overlay: it is the only
 *                           neutral that separates from that bar by more than a
 *                           rounding error -- 1.56:1 against #2A2F38, where the
 *                           surface immediately above it is 1.18:1 and the one
 *                           below 1.13:1, both invisible at icon-button size.
 *   bg.alt        #1E222A   alternating item-view row; halfway base -> raised
 *   ink           #16181D   text drawn ON an accent fill. 4.85:1 against
 *                           #E8543F; #EDF0F4 would be 3.19:1 and fail.
 *   ramp.hi       #F2F2F2   top of the texture-level value ramp
 *   ramp.lo       #101216   bottom of the same ramp
 *
 * And three alpha washes, which have no hex form because they must composite
 * over whatever surface they land on:
 *   accent.wash   rgba(232, 84, 63, 0.16)   quiet -- reserved
 *   accent.wash+  rgba(232, 84, 63, 0.26)   selected row, menu item, checked
 *   accent.wash++ rgba(232, 84, 63, 0.36)   selected AND hovered, text select
 * Measured on Qt 5.15.2: the fourth argument of rgba() accepts a 0-1 float, a
 * 0-255 integer AND a percentage, all three, and 0.26 renders identically to
 * 66 and to 26%. The float form is used here because it reads as a fraction.
 *
 * ===========================================================================
 * METRICS. One height, one radius, no gradients.
 * ===========================================================================
 *   control height   26px for QPushButton, QLineEdit, QComboBox and
 *                    QAbstractSpinBox. Getting there needs a DIFFERENT
 *                    vertical padding per class -- measured, not guessed; the
 *                    numbers and the reason are on the input rule below.
 *   corner radius     4px, everywhere, with one documented exception:
 *                    top-level popups (QMenu, combo popup, tool tip) stay
 *                    square, because a rounded top-level leaves the corner
 *                    pixels showing the desktop behind the window on Windows.
 *   accent bar        2px, border-left on the tool strip, border-bottom on
 *                    tabs. Reserved as transparent in every other state so
 *                    that becoming :checked does not reflow the icon.
 *   hairline          1px stroke.soft. These are structure, not decoration --
 *                    with the surfaces this close together they are the only
 *                    thing separating a panel from its neighbour.
 *
 * There is exactly ONE qlineargradient in this file, on the texture brush
 * level slider, where the groove is a black-to-white value ramp and therefore
 * data rather than chrome. No control is shaded.
 *
 * ===========================================================================
 * TYPE SCALE. Family is Segoe UI; nothing is bundled (see ATTRIBUTION.md).
 *   13/600  text.hi   dialog and window titles
 *   12/400  text      default control and label text  <- the global size
 *   12/600  text.hi   values that are being edited, active tab
 *   11/600  text.dim  section titles, dock titles, header sections
 *   11/400  text.dim  status bar, captions, units
 *   12 mono           QPlainTextEdit / QTextEdit (reports, Lua log, SQL)
 *
 * Qt QSS has NO text-transform and NO letter-spacing. Section titles are
 * therefore whatever case the .ui file typed; they are not upper-cased here
 * and cannot be. If they should read as small caps, the strings have to change
 * in the forms.
 *
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * The Font Awesome icon font is NOT shipped with this fork. Nothing in this
 * sheet fixes a control's size to a glyph, no state is signalled by an icon
 * alone, and the frameless title bar buttons get real PNGs via qproperty-icon
 * below -- so the window controls stay identifiable with no icon font at all.
 *
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * NOT REACHABLE FROM QSS -- for whoever wires the 3D side up in C++. Recorded
 * here so the viewport and the chrome stay one design.
 *   viewport.active     #F0A030   active object outline
 *   viewport.select     #E8632A   selected object outline
 *   spawn.creature      #3FD0A4   spawn.gameobject   #B98BE8
 *   waypoint.node/line  #FFC145   (line at 60% alpha)
 *   chunk.ghost         #E8543F   db.dirty  #E0A33E   db.deleted #E0574B @40%
 *   layer budget 1-2    #4FB477 @25%   3  #E0A33E @30%   4  #E0574B @35%
 * ======================================================================== */


/* ---------------------------------------------------------------- global -- */

QWidget {
	background-color: #1B1E24;
	color: #C6CCD6;
	border: none;
	font-family: "Segoe UI";
	font-size: 12px;
	outline: 0;
	selection-background-color: rgba(232, 84, 63, 0.26);
	selection-color: #EDF0F4;
}

QWidget:disabled {
	color: #59606B;
}

QDialog {
	background-color: #1B1E24;
}

QMessageBox {
	background-color: #1B1E24;
}

QMessageBox QLabel {
	color: #C6CCD6;
	font-size: 12px;
}

QLabel {
	background: transparent;
	color: #C6CCD6;
	padding: 1px 0px;
}

QLabel:disabled {
	color: #59606B;
}

/* Hyperlinks are one of the very few non-interaction uses of the accent. */
QLabel[openExternalLinks="true"] {
	color: #E8543F;
}

/* QFrame is a base class, so a bare QFrame rule would repaint every scroll
   area, tree, list and text edit in the app -- that is the seam the old Dark
   theme had. Only the two line shapes are targeted here. If your Qt build does
   not match enum-valued property selectors these two rules are simply ignored
   and separators stay as they are today. */
QFrame[frameShape="4"] {
	background-color: #262B33;
	border: none;
	max-height: 1px;
}

QFrame[frameShape="5"] {
	background-color: #262B33;
	border: none;
	max-width: 1px;
}


/* ------------------------------------------------- window, docks, gutters -- */

QMainWindow {
	background-color: #16181D;
}

/* The dock resize grips. Unstyled these are drawn by Fusion and are the
   lightest thing on screen. */
QMainWindow::separator {
	background-color: #16181D;
	width: 4px;
	height: 4px;
}

QMainWindow::separator:hover {
	background-color: #E8543F;
}

QSplitter {
	background: transparent;
	border: none;
}

QSplitter::handle {
	background-color: #16181D;
}

QSplitter::handle:horizontal {
	width: 4px;
}

QSplitter::handle:vertical {
	height: 4px;
}

QSplitter::handle:hover {
	background-color: #2A2F38;
}

QSplitter::handle:pressed {
	background-color: #E8543F;
}

QDockWidget {
	background-color: #1B1E24;
	color: #8A93A0;
	font-size: 11px;
	font-weight: 600;
	border: none;
	titlebar-close-icon: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/icon_close.png);
	titlebar-normal-icon: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/icon_restore.png);
}

/* Dock titles are chrome, not content: same surface as the panel they head,
   separated by a hairline rather than by being a lighter slab.

   They are NOT the same rank as a section heading, though, and until now they
   were drawn in the same 11/600 text.dim as every QGroupBox title inside the
   panel. The tool dock retitles itself to the active tool on every mode change
   (MapView sets the dock's window title from the tool's name), so this line is
   the app's answer to "which tool am I in" -- it has to out-rank the headings
   below it or it reads as one more divider. 13/600 text.hi plus the 2px accent
   bar, which is the same mark the exclusive tool strip uses, and for the same
   reason: this is the thing you are acting on.

   The bar is on the LEFT because a dock title is a horizontal band whose left
   edge is where the eye starts; a bottom bar here would collide with the
   hairline that already separates the title from the panel. */
QDockWidget::title {
	background-color: #1B1E24;
	color: #EDF0F4;
	border: none;
	border-left: 2px solid #E8543F;
	border-bottom: 1px solid #262B33;
	padding: 8px 8px 7px 10px;
	font-size: 13px;
	font-weight: 600;
	text-align: left;
}

QDockWidget::close-button,
QDockWidget::float-button {
	background: transparent;
	border: none;
	border-radius: 4px;
	width: 20px;
	height: 20px;
	padding: 0px;
	icon-size: 12px;
}

QDockWidget::close-button:hover,
QDockWidget::float-button:hover {
	background-color: #2A2F38;
}

QDockWidget::close-button:pressed,
QDockWidget::float-button:pressed {
	background-color: #B33F2E;
}


/* ------------------------------------------------------- menus, menu bar -- */

QMenuBar {
	background-color: #16181D;
	border: none;
	border-bottom: 1px solid #262B33;
	padding: 3px 6px;
	spacing: 2px;
}

QMenuBar::item {
	background: transparent;
	color: #C6CCD6;
	padding: 5px 10px;
	margin: 1px 1px;
	border: none;
	border-radius: 4px;
}

QMenuBar::item:selected {
	background-color: #2A2F38;
	color: #EDF0F4;
}

QMenuBar::item:pressed {
	background-color: rgba(232, 84, 63, 0.26);
	color: #EDF0F4;
}

QMenuBar::item:disabled {
	color: #59606B;
}

/* Square corners on purpose: a rounded top-level popup leaves the corner
   pixels showing whatever is behind the window on Windows. */
QMenu {
	background-color: #2A2F38;
	color: #C6CCD6;
	border: 1px solid #343A45;
	border-radius: 0px;
	padding: 5px 3px;
}

QMenu::item {
	background: transparent;
	color: #C6CCD6;
	padding: 6px 28px 6px 28px;
	margin: 1px 2px;
	border: none;
	border-radius: 4px;
}

/* A menu item has no :hover -- Qt marks the item under the cursor :selected --
   so the wash has to carry both meanings here. */
QMenu::item:selected {
	background-color: rgba(232, 84, 63, 0.26);
	color: #EDF0F4;
}

QMenu::item:disabled {
	background: transparent;
	color: #59606B;
}

QMenu::separator {
	height: 1px;
	background-color: #343A45;
	margin: 5px 12px;
}

QMenu::indicator {
	width: 14px;
	height: 14px;
	left: 8px;
}

QMenu::indicator:non-exclusive:checked,
QMenu::indicator:exclusive:checked {
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/icon_menu_check.png);
}

QMenu::indicator:non-exclusive:checked:disabled,
QMenu::indicator:exclusive:checked:disabled {
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/icon_menu_check_disabled.png);
}

QMenu::right-arrow {
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/right_arrow.png);
	width: 5px;
	height: 8px;
	margin-right: 9px;
}

QMenu::right-arrow:disabled {
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/right_arrow_disabled.png);
}

QMenu::scroller,
QMenu::tearoff {
	background-color: #2A2F38;
}


/* --------------------------------------------------------------- toolbar -- */

QToolBar {
	background-color: #22262E;
	border: none;
	border-bottom: 1px solid #262B33;
	spacing: 2px;
	padding: 4px;
}

QToolBar:vertical {
	border-bottom: none;
	border-right: 1px solid #262B33;
}

QToolBar::separator {
	background-color: #343A45;
	width: 1px;
	height: 1px;
	margin: 5px 4px;
}

QToolBar::handle:horizontal {
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/handle_vertical.png);
	width: 8px;
	margin: 2px 1px;
}

QToolBar::handle:vertical {
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/handle_horizontal.png);
	height: 8px;
	margin: 1px 2px;
}


/* ----------------------------------------------------------- status bar -- */

/* The status bar carries tile coordinates, client and server position, zone,
   in-game time, FPS and the loaded/rendered counts. It is chrome reporting on
   content, so it takes the recessed surface and a hairline lid.

   It carries SEVEN separate readouts, every one of them a bare QLabel with no
   object name and no dynamic property -- MapView builds _status_position,
   _status_selection, _status_area, _status_time, _status_fps, _status_culling
   and _status_database and hands each straight to addWidget. There is nothing
   in the widget tree for a selector to grab hold of, so this sheet cannot make
   one readout out-rank another. What it CAN do is stop them running together:

     * a hairline before each field, which turns one long sentence into seven
       addressable slots. QSS has no :first-child, so the leftmost field also
       gets a leading rule; it lands against the window edge where it reads as
       part of the frame.
     * the readouts at text (11.0:1 on bg.sunken) rather than text.dim (5.72:1),
       because they are the content of the bar, not its caption. The transient
       showMessage() text QStatusBar paints itself stays at text.dim, so a
       five-second "Map saved" no longer looks louder than the position.

   The per-field emphasis still needs one line of C++ each: the
   QLabel[state="value"] rule further down is the mechanism and nothing in src/
   calls setProperty("state", ...), so it has never fired. That rule carries an
   attribute selector and therefore outranks the plain descendant rule below --
   wire it up on FPS, position and the loaded/rendered counts and they lift to
   text.hi semibold without anything here changing. Set it once at
   construction, before the first polish, and no unpolish/polish is needed. */
QStatusBar {
	background-color: #16181D;
	color: #8A93A0;
	border: none;
	border-top: 1px solid #262B33;
	font-size: 11px;
	min-height: 24px;
	padding: 0px 2px;
}

QStatusBar::item {
	border: none;
}

QStatusBar QLabel {
	color: #C6CCD6;
	border-left: 1px solid #262B33;
	font-size: 11px;
	padding: 1px 10px;
}

QStatusBar QLabel:disabled {
	color: #59606B;
}

QSizeGrip {
	background: transparent;
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/sizegrip.png);
	width: 14px;
	height: 14px;
}


/* --------------------------------------------------------------- buttons -- */

QPushButton {
	background-color: #22262E;
	color: #C6CCD6;
	border: 1px solid #343A45;
	border-radius: 4px;
	padding: 4px 10px;
	min-height: 16px;
}

QPushButton:hover {
	background-color: #2A2F38;
	border: 1px solid #454C59;
	color: #EDF0F4;
}

QPushButton:pressed {
	background-color: #B33F2E;
	border: 1px solid #B33F2E;
	color: #EDF0F4;
}

QPushButton:checked {
	background-color: rgba(232, 84, 63, 0.26);
	border: 1px solid #E8543F;
	color: #EDF0F4;
}

QPushButton:checked:hover {
	background-color: rgba(232, 84, 63, 0.36);
	border: 1px solid #E8543F;
}

/* Focus is a border colour change and nothing else -- no glow, no outline, no
   size change, so nothing reflows when focus moves. */
QPushButton:focus {
	border: 1px solid #E8543F;
}

QPushButton:default {
	border: 1px solid #E8543F;
	color: #EDF0F4;
}

QPushButton:disabled {
	background-color: #1B1E24;
	color: #59606B;
	border: 1px solid #262B33;
}

QPushButton:flat {
	background: transparent;
	border: 1px solid transparent;
}

QPushButton:flat:hover {
	background-color: #2A2F38;
	border: 1px solid transparent;
}

QPushButton:flat:pressed {
	background-color: #B33F2E;
	color: #EDF0F4;
}

QPushButton::menu-indicator {
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/down_arrow.png);
	width: 8px;
	height: 5px;
	subcontrol-origin: padding;
	subcontrol-position: center right;
	right: 6px;
}

QDialogButtonBox QPushButton {
	min-width: 84px;
	padding: 4px 16px;
}

/* Destructive actions. Set from C++ with  btn->setProperty("state", "danger");
   and, if the button already existed, style()->unpolish(btn); polish(btn); */
QPushButton[state="danger"] {
	background-color: #2A1B1B;
	color: #E0574B;
	border: 1px solid #5E2C29;
}

QPushButton[state="danger"]:hover {
	background-color: #E0574B;
	color: #16181D;
	border: 1px solid #E0574B;
}

QPushButton[state="danger"]:pressed {
	background-color: #B8443A;
	color: #16181D;
}


/* ----------------------------------------------------------- tool buttons -- */

/* The tool strip down the left edge and the toolbar across the top. Flat, no
   border, no box -- the ONLY thing that marks the active tool is a 2px accent
   bar on the leading edge plus the raised hover surface. The bar is reserved
   as transparent in every state so switching tools does not shift the icon by
   two pixels. */
QToolButton {
	background: transparent;
	color: #C6CCD6;
	border: 1px solid transparent;
	border-left: 2px solid transparent;
	border-radius: 4px;
	padding: 4px 6px;
	min-width: 24px;
	min-height: 24px;
}

QToolButton:hover {
	background-color: #2A2F38;
	color: #EDF0F4;
	border: 1px solid transparent;
	border-left: 2px solid transparent;
}

QToolButton:pressed {
	background-color: #2A2F38;
	border-left: 2px solid #B33F2E;
}

/* Checked is an accent WASH plus the bar, not bg.overlay plus the bar. Checked
   against the running editor: bg.overlay on a bg.raised toolbar is a 1.13
   contrast step, which at icon-button size is invisible, so the bar was left
   carrying the whole signal -- and a 2px vertical line between two icons reads
   as a SEPARATOR, not as state. The wash makes the button itself the mark and
   demotes the bar to its edge.

   This rule is now the EXCLUSIVE-SELECTION look: the left tool strip, whose
   actions share one QActionGroup, so exactly one of them is checked and accent
   means "the tool you are editing with". The independent view toggles are
   overridden by class near the end of the file.

   The obvious way to write that override would have been
       QToolBar:horizontal QToolButton:checked
   and it does not work. QCss::Selector::pseudoClass() reads
   basicSelectors.last() and nothing else, and QCss::StyleSelector
   (qcssparser_p.h:654-687) exposes virtuals for a node's name, ids and
   attributes but NONE for its pseudo-state -- there is no hook with which an
   ancestor's :horizontal could ever be evaluated. Qt parses the selector,
   silently drops the ancestor pseudo, and the rule then matches every toolbar
   in the app including this one. Ancestors may be matched by class name, by
   #objectName or by [attribute]; never by pseudo-state. check_qss.py fails the
   sheet if one is reintroduced. */
QToolButton:checked {
	background-color: rgba(232, 84, 63, 0.26);
	color: #EDF0F4;
	border: 1px solid transparent;
	border-left: 2px solid #E8543F;
}

QToolButton:checked:hover {
	background-color: rgba(232, 84, 63, 0.36);
	border-left: 2px solid #E8543F;
}

QToolButton:disabled {
	background: transparent;
	color: #59606B;
	border: 1px solid transparent;
	border-left: 2px solid transparent;
}

QToolButton[popupMode="1"] {
	padding-right: 18px;
}

QToolButton::menu-button {
	background: transparent;
	border: none;
	border-left: 1px solid #343A45;
	width: 14px;
}

QToolButton::menu-button:hover {
	background-color: #343A45;
}

QToolButton::menu-arrow,
QToolButton::menu-indicator {
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/down_arrow.png);
	width: 8px;
	height: 5px;
}

QToolButton::menu-arrow:disabled,
QToolButton::menu-indicator:disabled {
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/down_arrow_disabled.png);
}


/* ---------------------------------------------------------------- inputs -- */

/* Every text-entry control is one shape: 26px tall, 4px radius, bg.raised,
   1px stroke. Focus turns the stroke accent and does nothing else.

   The three classes need THREE DIFFERENT vertical paddings to arrive at the
   same 26px, because their size hints do not agree about what min-height
   means. Measured on Qt 5.15.2, font-size 12px, 1px border:
     QPushButton     h = min-height + 2 + 2*vpad     -> mh 16, vpad 4
     QLineEdit       h = 26 + 2*vpad, min-height IGNORED below its own floor
     QAbstractSpinBox  as QLineEdit                  -> vpad 0
     QComboBox       h = 23 + vpad_top + vpad_bottom, min-height IGNORED,
                     so an even total can only reach 25 or 27 -- the 2px/1px
                     split below is what lands it exactly on 26.
   Change a padding here and you have changed the height; re-measure. */
QLineEdit,
QTextEdit,
QPlainTextEdit,
QAbstractSpinBox,
QComboBox {
	background-color: #22262E;
	color: #EDF0F4;
	border: 1px solid #343A45;
	border-radius: 4px;
	selection-background-color: rgba(232, 84, 63, 0.36);
	selection-color: #EDF0F4;
}

QLineEdit,
QAbstractSpinBox {
	padding: 0px 8px;
	min-height: 16px;
}

QLineEdit:hover,
QTextEdit:hover,
QPlainTextEdit:hover,
QAbstractSpinBox:hover,
QComboBox:hover {
	border: 1px solid #454C59;
}

QLineEdit:focus,
QTextEdit:focus,
QPlainTextEdit:focus,
QAbstractSpinBox:focus,
QComboBox:focus {
	background-color: #2A2F38;
	border: 1px solid #E8543F;
}

QLineEdit:disabled,
QTextEdit:disabled,
QPlainTextEdit:disabled,
QAbstractSpinBox:disabled,
QComboBox:disabled {
	background-color: #1B1E24;
	color: #59606B;
	border: 1px solid #262B33;
}

QLineEdit:read-only {
	background-color: #1B1E24;
	color: #8A93A0;
	border: 1px solid #262B33;
}

/* Reports, the Lua console log and any SQL preview want tabular figures. */
QTextEdit,
QPlainTextEdit {
	font-family: "Consolas";
	font-size: 12px;
	padding: 8px;
}

/* Error and warning states on an input. Set from C++ with
   setProperty("state", "error") / ("state", "warn") plus unpolish/polish;
   the colour stays here, the semantics stay in the code. */
QLineEdit[state="error"],
QAbstractSpinBox[state="error"],
QComboBox[state="error"] {
	border: 1px solid #E0574B;
}

QLineEdit[state="warn"],
QAbstractSpinBox[state="warn"],
QComboBox[state="warn"] {
	border: 1px solid #E0A33E;
}

QLabel[state="error"] {
	color: #E0574B;
}

QLabel[state="warn"] {
	color: #E0A33E;
}

QLabel[state="ok"] {
	color: #4FB477;
}

/* A label carrying a live value -- the status bar readouts, the current radius,
   a spawn GUID. Lifted out of the label text around it. */
QLabel[state="value"] {
	color: #EDF0F4;
	font-weight: 600;
}


/* ------------------------------------------------------------ spin boxes -- */

/* The steppers are a strip inside the field rather than two chiclets bolted to
   it: no fill of their own until hovered, one hairline dividing them off. */
QAbstractSpinBox {
	padding-right: 18px;
}

QAbstractSpinBox::up-button {
	subcontrol-origin: border;
	subcontrol-position: top right;
	background: transparent;
	border: none;
	border-left: 1px solid #343A45;
	border-top-right-radius: 3px;
	width: 15px;
	margin: 1px 1px 0px 0px;
}

QAbstractSpinBox::down-button {
	subcontrol-origin: border;
	subcontrol-position: bottom right;
	background: transparent;
	border: none;
	border-left: 1px solid #343A45;
	border-bottom-right-radius: 3px;
	width: 15px;
	margin: 0px 1px 1px 0px;
}

QAbstractSpinBox::up-button:hover,
QAbstractSpinBox::down-button:hover {
	background-color: #2A2F38;
}

QAbstractSpinBox::up-button:pressed,
QAbstractSpinBox::down-button:pressed {
	background-color: #B33F2E;
}

QAbstractSpinBox::up-button:disabled,
QAbstractSpinBox::down-button:disabled {
	background: transparent;
	border-left: 1px solid #262B33;
}

QAbstractSpinBox::up-arrow {
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/up_arrow.png);
	width: 8px;
	height: 5px;
}

QAbstractSpinBox::down-arrow {
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/down_arrow.png);
	width: 8px;
	height: 5px;
}

QAbstractSpinBox::up-arrow:disabled,
QAbstractSpinBox::up-arrow:off {
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/up_arrow_disabled.png);
}

QAbstractSpinBox::down-arrow:disabled,
QAbstractSpinBox::down-arrow:off {
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/down_arrow_disabled.png);
}


/* -------------------------------------------------------------- combo box -- */

/* 2px top / 1px bottom is not a typo -- see the note on the input rule. It is
   the only split that makes a combo exactly as tall as the button next to it. */
QComboBox {
	padding: 2px 24px 1px 8px;
	min-height: 16px;
}

QComboBox:on {
	border: 1px solid #E8543F;
}

QComboBox::drop-down {
	subcontrol-origin: border;
	subcontrol-position: top right;
	background: transparent;
	border: none;
	border-left: 1px solid #343A45;
	border-top-right-radius: 3px;
	border-bottom-right-radius: 3px;
	width: 18px;
	margin: 1px;
}

QComboBox::drop-down:hover {
	background-color: #2A2F38;
}

QComboBox::drop-down:disabled {
	background: transparent;
	border-left: 1px solid #262B33;
}

QComboBox::down-arrow {
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/down_arrow.png);
	width: 8px;
	height: 5px;
}

QComboBox::down-arrow:on {
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/up_arrow.png);
}

QComboBox::down-arrow:disabled {
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/down_arrow_disabled.png);
}

/* The popup is a separate top-level widget and does not inherit the combo's
   own rule, so it has to be styled by name. */
QComboBox QAbstractItemView {
	background-color: #2A2F38;
	color: #C6CCD6;
	border: 1px solid #343A45;
	border-radius: 0px;
	padding: 3px;
	outline: 0;
	selection-background-color: rgba(232, 84, 63, 0.26);
	selection-color: #EDF0F4;
}


/* ------------------------------------------------- check boxes and radios -- */

/* The nine-radio brush type grid is the most prominent control in the editor.
   The indicator is a plain 16px circle on bg.raised with a stroke ring,
   flooded accent with an ink dot when checked -- no bevel, no inner shadow,
   nothing that needs a glyph font to be legible. */
QCheckBox,
QRadioButton {
	background: transparent;
	color: #C6CCD6;
	spacing: 8px;
	padding: 3px 0px;
	border: none;
}

QCheckBox:hover,
QRadioButton:hover {
	color: #EDF0F4;
}

QCheckBox:checked,
QRadioButton:checked {
	color: #EDF0F4;
}

QCheckBox:disabled,
QRadioButton:disabled {
	color: #59606B;
}

QCheckBox::indicator,
QRadioButton::indicator,
QGroupBox::indicator {
	width: 16px;
	height: 16px;
}

QCheckBox::indicator:unchecked,
QGroupBox::indicator:unchecked {
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/icon_checkbox_unchecked.png);
}

QCheckBox::indicator:unchecked:hover,
QGroupBox::indicator:unchecked:hover {
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/icon_checkbox_unchecked_hover.png);
}

QCheckBox::indicator:unchecked:disabled,
QGroupBox::indicator:unchecked:disabled {
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/icon_checkbox_unchecked_disabled.png);
}

QCheckBox::indicator:checked,
QGroupBox::indicator:checked {
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/icon_checkbox_checked.png);
}

QCheckBox::indicator:checked:hover,
QGroupBox::indicator:checked:hover {
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/icon_checkbox_checked_hover.png);
}

QCheckBox::indicator:checked:disabled,
QGroupBox::indicator:checked:disabled {
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/icon_checkbox_checked_disabled.png);
}

QCheckBox::indicator:indeterminate {
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/icon_checkbox_indeterminate.png);
}

QCheckBox::indicator:indeterminate:disabled {
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/icon_checkbox_indeterminate_disabled.png);
}

QRadioButton::indicator:unchecked {
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/icon_radiobutton_unchecked.png);
}

QRadioButton::indicator:unchecked:hover {
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/icon_radiobutton_unchecked_hover.png);
}

QRadioButton::indicator:unchecked:disabled {
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/icon_radiobutton_unchecked_disabled.png);
}

QRadioButton::indicator:checked {
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/icon_radiobutton_checked.png);
}

QRadioButton::indicator:checked:hover {
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/icon_radiobutton_checked_hover.png);
}

QRadioButton::indicator:checked:disabled {
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/icon_radiobutton_checked_disabled.png);
}


/* -------------------------------------------------------------- group box -- */

/* THE BIG ONE. Stock Qt draws a group box as a bordered rectangle with a
   centred bold underlined title, and the tool panels are nothing but group
   boxes, so the right-hand dock reads as a stack of 1998 dialog frames. Here
   there is no box at all: the title is a small dim caption sitting top-left in
   the widget's own top margin, one hairline runs under it, and that hairline
   is the only mark on screen. Everything below it is just spacing.

   Mechanically: margin-top opens 20px above the frame for the title to occupy,
   border-top paints the rule at the top of the frame -- i.e. directly under
   the title -- and padding-bottom is the gap to the next section. */
QGroupBox {
	background: transparent;
	border: none;
	border-top: 1px solid #262B33;
	border-radius: 0px;
	margin-top: 20px;
	margin-bottom: 4px;
	padding: 12px 0px 14px 0px;
	color: #8A93A0;
	font-size: 11px;
	font-weight: 600;
	spacing: 8px;
}

QGroupBox::title {
	subcontrol-origin: margin;
	subcontrol-position: top left;
	background: transparent;
	color: #8A93A0;
	left: 0px;
	padding: 0px 8px 6px 0px;
}

QGroupBox::title:disabled {
	color: #59606B;
}

QGroupBox:disabled {
	border-top: 1px solid #22262E;
}

/* A group box nested inside another one gets no rule of its own, or the panel
   turns into a ladder. */
QGroupBox QGroupBox {
	border-top: none;
	margin-top: 16px;
	padding-top: 4px;
}

/* A checkable group box needs its indicator clear of the title text. */
QGroupBox::indicator {
	margin-right: 6px;
}


/* ------------------------------------------------------------------- tabs -- */

/* No raised boxes and no pane frame: the selected tab is marked by a 2px
   accent rule on its own edge, and the pane is separated from the strip by a
   single hairline. */
QTabWidget::pane {
	background-color: #1B1E24;
	border: none;
	border-top: 1px solid #262B33;
	border-radius: 0px;
	top: -1px;
}

QTabWidget::tab-bar {
	left: 0px;
}

QTabBar {
	background: transparent;
	border: none;
	qproperty-drawBase: 0;
}

QTabBar::tab {
	background: transparent;
	color: #8A93A0;
	border: none;
	border-bottom: 2px solid transparent;
	padding: 7px 14px;
	min-width: 46px;
	font-size: 12px;
}

QTabBar::tab:hover {
	background-color: #22262E;
	color: #EDF0F4;
}

QTabBar::tab:selected {
	background: transparent;
	color: #EDF0F4;
	border-bottom: 2px solid #E8543F;
	font-weight: 600;
}

QTabBar::tab:disabled {
	color: #59606B;
}

QTabBar::tab:left,
QTabBar::tab:right {
	border-bottom: none;
	padding: 14px 7px;
	min-width: 0px;
	min-height: 46px;
}

QTabBar::tab:left {
	border-right: 2px solid transparent;
}

QTabBar::tab:left:selected {
	border-right: 2px solid #E8543F;
}

QTabBar::tab:right {
	border-left: 2px solid transparent;
}

QTabBar::tab:right:selected {
	border-left: 2px solid #E8543F;
}

QTabBar::close-button {
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/icon_close.png);
	subcontrol-position: right;
	margin: 2px;
}

QTabBar::close-button:hover {
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/icon_close_hover.png);
}

QTabBar QToolButton {
	background-color: #22262E;
	border: 1px solid #262B33;
	border-left: 1px solid #262B33;
}


/* ---------------------------------------------------------------- toolbox -- */

QToolBox {
	background: transparent;
	border: none;
}

QToolBox::tab {
	background: transparent;
	color: #8A93A0;
	border: none;
	border-left: 2px solid transparent;
	border-bottom: 1px solid #262B33;
	padding: 8px 10px;
	font-size: 11px;
	font-weight: 600;
}

QToolBox::tab:hover {
	background-color: #22262E;
	color: #EDF0F4;
}

QToolBox::tab:selected {
	background-color: #22262E;
	color: #EDF0F4;
	border-left: 2px solid #E8543F;
}

QToolBox::tab:disabled {
	color: #59606B;
}


/* ------------------------------------------------------------- item views -- */

/* Selection is an accent WASH, never a saturated fill. In the texture and
   asset browsers the cell contents are the data; flooding them would hide the
   very thing that was picked. */
QAbstractItemView {
	background-color: #22262E;
	alternate-background-color: #1E222A;
	color: #C6CCD6;
	border: 1px solid #262B33;
	border-radius: 4px;
	padding: 3px;
	outline: 0;
	show-decoration-selected: 1;
	selection-background-color: rgba(232, 84, 63, 0.26);
	selection-color: #EDF0F4;
}

QAbstractItemView:disabled {
	background-color: #1B1E24;
	color: #59606B;
	border: 1px solid #262B33;
}

QAbstractItemView::item {
	background: transparent;
	border: none;
	padding: 5px 6px;
	min-height: 18px;
}

QAbstractItemView::item:hover {
	background-color: #2A2F38;
	color: #EDF0F4;
}

/* Four distinct selection states, not one. Without :!active a panel that has
   lost focus still screams "this row is selected" as loudly as the one you are
   actually working in. */
QAbstractItemView::item:selected:active {
	background-color: rgba(232, 84, 63, 0.26);
	color: #EDF0F4;
}

QAbstractItemView::item:selected:!active {
	background-color: #2A2F38;
	color: #C6CCD6;
}

QAbstractItemView::item:selected:hover {
	background-color: rgba(232, 84, 63, 0.36);
	color: #EDF0F4;
}

QAbstractItemView::item:disabled {
	color: #59606B;
}

QListView {
	background-color: #22262E;
	border: 1px solid #262B33;
}

QListView::item {
	min-height: 18px;
}

QTreeView {
	background-color: #22262E;
	border: 1px solid #262B33;
	paint-alternating-row-colors-for-empty-area: 0;
}

QTreeView::item {
	padding: 4px 2px;
}

/* No dotted connector lines. The chevron alone is the affordance, which is
   what every modern tree does and what survives at small sizes. */
QTreeView::branch {
	background: transparent;
	border-image: none;
	image: none;
}

QTreeView::branch:has-children:!has-siblings:closed,
QTreeView::branch:closed:has-children:has-siblings {
	border-image: none;
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/icon_branch_closed.png);
}

QTreeView::branch:open:has-children:!has-siblings,
QTreeView::branch:open:has-children:has-siblings {
	border-image: none;
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/icon_branch_open.png);
}

QTreeView::branch:selected {
	background-color: rgba(232, 84, 63, 0.26);
}

QTableView {
	background-color: #22262E;
	alternate-background-color: #1E222A;
	gridline-color: #262B33;
	border: 1px solid #262B33;
	selection-background-color: rgba(232, 84, 63, 0.26);
	selection-color: #EDF0F4;
}

QTableView QTableCornerButton::section {
	background-color: #1B1E24;
	border: none;
	border-right: 1px solid #262B33;
	border-bottom: 1px solid #262B33;
}

QHeaderView {
	background: transparent;
	border: none;
}

QHeaderView::section {
	background-color: #1B1E24;
	color: #8A93A0;
	border: none;
	border-right: 1px solid #262B33;
	border-bottom: 1px solid #262B33;
	padding: 6px 8px;
	font-size: 11px;
	font-weight: 600;
}

QHeaderView::section:hover {
	background-color: #2A2F38;
	color: #EDF0F4;
}

QHeaderView::section:checked {
	background-color: #22262E;
	color: #EDF0F4;
	border-bottom: 2px solid #E8543F;
}

QHeaderView::section:disabled {
	color: #59606B;
}

QHeaderView::section:last,
QHeaderView::section:only-one {
	border-right: none;
}

QHeaderView::down-arrow {
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/down_arrow.png);
	width: 8px;
	height: 5px;
	subcontrol-origin: content;
	subcontrol-position: center right;
	right: 4px;
}

QHeaderView::up-arrow {
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/up_arrow.png);
	width: 8px;
	height: 5px;
	subcontrol-origin: content;
	subcontrol-position: center right;
	right: 4px;
}


/* ------------------------------------------------------------ scroll bars -- */

/* 10px, no arrows, no track. The trough is the recessed surface so it
   disappears against the chrome; only the thumb is visible, and only the thumb
   moves. */
QScrollBar:vertical {
	background-color: #16181D;
	border: none;
	width: 10px;
	margin: 0px;
}

QScrollBar:horizontal {
	background-color: #16181D;
	border: none;
	height: 10px;
	margin: 0px;
}

QScrollBar::handle:vertical {
	background-color: #343A45;
	border: none;
	border-radius: 3px;
	margin: 2px;
	min-height: 28px;
}

QScrollBar::handle:horizontal {
	background-color: #343A45;
	border: none;
	border-radius: 3px;
	margin: 2px;
	min-width: 28px;
}

QScrollBar::handle:hover {
	background-color: #454C59;
}

QScrollBar::handle:pressed {
	background-color: #E8543F;
}

QScrollBar::handle:disabled {
	background-color: #22262E;
}

QScrollBar::add-line,
QScrollBar::sub-line {
	background: none;
	border: none;
	width: 0px;
	height: 0px;
}

QScrollBar::add-page,
QScrollBar::sub-page {
	background: transparent;
}

QScrollBar::up-arrow,
QScrollBar::down-arrow,
QScrollBar::left-arrow,
QScrollBar::right-arrow {
	background: none;
	image: none;
	width: 0px;
	height: 0px;
}

QAbstractScrollArea::corner {
	background-color: #16181D;
	border: none;
}


/* --------------------------------------------------------------- sliders -- */

/* Every Radius / Inner Radius / Speed row in the tool panels ends in one of
   these, so it is the most repeated control in the editor after the label.
   The filled portion is accent: the groove is a readout, not a rail. */
QSlider {
	background: transparent;
	border: none;
}

QSlider::groove:horizontal {
	background-color: #16181D;
	border: none;
	border-radius: 2px;
	height: 4px;
	margin: 0px 2px;
}

QSlider::sub-page:horizontal {
	background-color: #E8543F;
	border: none;
	border-radius: 2px;
}

QSlider::add-page:horizontal {
	background-color: #16181D;
	border: none;
	border-radius: 2px;
}

QSlider::handle:horizontal {
	background-color: #C6CCD6;
	border: none;
	border-radius: 2px;
	width: 6px;
	height: 16px;
	margin: -6px 0px;
}

QSlider::groove:vertical {
	background-color: #16181D;
	border: none;
	border-radius: 2px;
	width: 4px;
	margin: 2px 0px;
}

QSlider::sub-page:vertical {
	background-color: #16181D;
	border: none;
	border-radius: 2px;
}

QSlider::add-page:vertical {
	background-color: #E8543F;
	border: none;
	border-radius: 2px;
}

QSlider::handle:vertical {
	background-color: #C6CCD6;
	border: none;
	border-radius: 2px;
	height: 6px;
	width: 16px;
	margin: 0px -6px;
}

QSlider::handle:hover {
	background-color: #EDF0F4;
}

QSlider::handle:pressed {
	background-color: #E8543F;
}

QSlider::handle:disabled {
	background-color: #343A45;
}

QSlider::sub-page:horizontal:disabled,
QSlider::add-page:vertical:disabled {
	background-color: #343A45;
}


/* ---------------------------------------------------------- progress bar -- */

QProgressBar {
	background-color: #16181D;
	color: #C6CCD6;
	border: none;
	border-radius: 4px;
	text-align: center;
	font-size: 11px;
	min-height: 18px;
}

QProgressBar::chunk {
	background-color: #E8543F;
	border: none;
	border-radius: 4px;
}

QProgressBar:disabled {
	color: #59606B;
}

QProgressBar::chunk:disabled {
	background-color: #343A45;
}


/* -------------------------------------------------------------- tool tip -- */

/* The single most jarring thing about the shipped themes was a light system
   tool tip on a dark app: neither of them had a QToolTip rule at all. */
QToolTip {
	background-color: #2A2F38;
	color: #EDF0F4;
	border: 1px solid #343A45;
	border-radius: 0px;
	padding: 5px 8px;
	font-size: 12px;
}


/* ---------------------------------------------------- scroll area seams -- */

/* QScrollArea, its viewport and its content widget are three separate widgets.
   Left alone they pick up three different backgrounds and you get a visible
   seam down the middle of every tool panel. Item views are QAbstractScrollArea,
   not QScrollArea, so they keep their own surface above. */
QScrollArea {
	background: transparent;
	border: none;
}

QScrollArea > QWidget > QWidget {
	background: transparent;
}

/* The right-hand tool dock. This used to claim it was what stopped every
   control in the panel from touching the dock edge, and it was not: the only
   thing in the tree that sets the object name "dockWidgetScrollArea" is the
   Advanced Docking System (external/QtAdvancedDockingSystem/src/DockWidget.cpp),
   which is linked but never instantiated -- see the ADS block at the bottom.
   The rule has therefore never matched anything.

   The scroll area the tool dock actually builds comes from
   src/noggit/ui/tools/ToolPanel/ToolPanelScroll.ui, where it is named
   "scrollArea" and its content widget "scrollAreaWidgetContents". Both names
   are unique in this tree. All four layout margins in that form are 0, so the
   panel has genuinely had no horizontal breathing room.

   The ADS name is kept so the rule is still there if ADS is ever switched on.
   HONEST LIMIT: whether Qt honours `padding` as a layout inset on a
   QScrollArea / plain QWidget is not something this sheet can prove, and it
   was not verified against a running editor. If it does nothing it costs
   nothing -- but do not read this rule as evidence that the padding is
   applied. The reliable fix is contentsMargins in the form. */
QScrollArea#dockWidgetScrollArea,
QScrollArea#scrollArea {
	background: transparent;
	border: none;
	padding: 0px 4px;
}

QWidget#scrollAreaWidgetContents {
	background: transparent;
	padding: 0px 4px;
}


/* ================================================================ Noggit == */
/* Application-specific selectors. Class names use Qt's convention of "--" for
   "::", so Noggit::Ui::PaletteList is Noggit--Ui--PaletteList. The shipped
   themes spelled these with a single dash in the first position, which matches
   nothing; both spellings are listed here so neither is silently dead.       */

/* --- the frameless window title bar ------------------------------------- */

QLabel[accessibleName="titlebar_icon"],
QPushButton[accessibleName="titlebar_icon"] {
	background: transparent;
	border: none;
	margin-left: 8px;
	margin-right: 6px;
}

QPushButton[accessibleName="titlebar_minimize"],
QPushButton[accessibleName="titlebar_maximize"],
QPushButton[accessibleName="titlebar_close"] {
	background: transparent;
	border: none;
	border-radius: 0px;
	min-width: 36px;
	padding: 0px;
}

QPushButton[accessibleName="titlebar_minimize"]:hover,
QPushButton[accessibleName="titlebar_maximize"]:hover {
	background-color: #2A2F38;
}

QPushButton[accessibleName="titlebar_minimize"]:pressed,
QPushButton[accessibleName="titlebar_maximize"]:pressed {
	background-color: #343A45;
}

/* Close is the one control that goes fully saturated on hover, because it is
   the one control you cannot undo. */
QPushButton[accessibleName="titlebar_close"]:hover {
	background-color: #E0574B;
}

QPushButton[accessibleName="titlebar_close"]:pressed {
	background-color: #B8443A;
}

/* Real PNG icons rather than Font Awesome glyphs, so the window controls are
   identifiable with no icon font installed. The maximize button never swaps
   its icon in code (it only changes its tool tip), so a static icon is safe. */
QPushButton[accessibleName="titlebar_minimize"] {
	qproperty-icon: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/icon_window_minimize.png);
	qproperty-iconSize: 12px 12px;
}

QPushButton[accessibleName="titlebar_maximize"] {
	qproperty-icon: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/icon_window_maximize.png);
	qproperty-iconSize: 11px 11px;
}

QPushButton[accessibleName="titlebar_close"] {
	qproperty-icon: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/icon_window_close.png);
	qproperty-iconSize: 12px 12px;
}

/* --- icon-font buttons --------------------------------------------------- */

/* These two accessible names exist to tint the icon fonts. Font Awesome is not
   shipped, so this often colours a short text fallback instead of a glyph;
   either way it must be neutral -- an icon is not a link and not a selection. */
QWidget[accessibleName="FontNoggitButtonStyle"],
QWidget[accessibleName="FontAwesomeButtonStyle"] {
	color: #C6CCD6;
}

QWidget[accessibleName="FontNoggitButtonStyle"]:disabled,
QWidget[accessibleName="FontAwesomeButtonStyle"]:disabled {
	color: #59606B;
}

/* --- the viewport overlay ------------------------------------------------ */

/* The overlay itself is translucent, but its layout holders are plain QWidgets
   and would otherwise paint opaque slabs of panel colour over the 3D view. */
QWidget#MapViewOverlay,
QWidget#leftToolbarHolder,
QWidget#upperToolbarHolder,
QWidget#secondaryToolbarHolder,
QWidget#leftSecondaryToolbarHolder {
	background: transparent;
}

/* Floating bars sit on top of the render. QSS has no box-shadow; the border
   and the lighter fill are what separate them from the viewport behind.

   KNOWN DEAD, LEFT IN PLACE: Noggit--Ui--toolbar (the left tool strip) matches
   nothing. Qt resolves a QSS type selector against metaObject()->className()
   and its superclass chain, and Noggit::Ui::toolbar carries no Q_OBJECT macro
   (src/noggit/ui/Toolbar.h) -- there is no moc_Toolbar.cpp among the 580
   generated in build/noggit_autogen, so its className is literally "QToolBar".
   The left strip consequently falls through to the plain QToolBar rule further
   up and is drawn as a docked bar rather than a floating one.

   The type selector is still dead and is still kept. What has changed is that
   the strip is now reached by POSITION instead, immediately below: the holder
   it lives in does have an object name, and MapView::setupToolbars puts
   exactly one widget in it (src/noggit/MapView.cpp -- a QVBoxLayout on
   _viewport_overlay_ui->leftToolbarHolder with _toolbar as its only member),
   so the child selector cannot reach anything else. The one-line Q_OBJECT fix
   in Toolbar.h remains the tidier answer and is deliberately NOT taken here,
   because it depends on whether AUTOMOC has that header in its scan set --
   a CMake question this change is not allowed to answer. Reaching the strip
   through its holder needs no build system at all, and outranks both the
   plain QToolBar rule (specificity 1) and QToolBar:vertical (11) at 102, so
   it wins by cascade rather than by document order. */
Noggit--Ui--toolbar,
QWidget#leftToolbarHolder > QToolBar,
Noggit--Ui--Tools--ViewToolbar--Ui--ViewToolbar,
QWidget#gizmoBar {
	background-color: #2A2F38;
	border: 1px solid #343A45;
	border-radius: 4px;
	padding: 4px;
	spacing: 2px;
}

/* --- the left tool strip ------------------------------------------------- */

/* Sixteen editing tools down the left edge of the viewport, in one
   QActionGroup, so exactly one is checked at a time.

   THE CARD is the holder selector in the shared floating-bar rule above, NOT
   anything in this block. Without it the strip took QToolBar #22262E plus
   QToolBar:vertical's 1px #262B33 right edge, so the most prominent chrome in
   the editor rendered as an opaque square-cornered slab forty pixels from a
   rounded, bordered, lighter floating card carrying the view toggles. Same
   surface, same distance from the camera, two design languages.

   No rule here cancels the plain QToolBar edges, and none is needed: the
   shared rule's `border: 1px solid #343A45` is a four-edge shorthand at
   specificity 65538 against 257 for QToolBar:vertical and 1 for QToolBar, so
   it replaces both. A "border-bottom: none" tidy-up placed after it would
   have been read as removing the card's own bottom edge, which is worse than
   the problem it was written for.

   HOVER had to be restated. The base QToolButton:hover fills with bg.overlay
   #2A2F38, which is exactly what the strip's surface has just become, so
   pointing at a tool would have measured 1.000:1 -- the identical defect the
   view-toggle bar block further down documents at length. It takes the same
   answer: the stroke.hi plate at 1.556:1 with a 1px text.off edge at 2.119:1.

   CHECKED. The base rule's accent wash is rgba(232, 84, 63, 0.26), authored
   against bg.overlay; over the slab the strip used to be it composited to
   #553232 and measured 1.37:1, which this sheet's own standard calls
   invisible at icon-button size. Raised to 0.36 it composites over the card
   and measures 1.52:1, in the same band as the hover plate the sheet already
   accepts. The 2px accent leading edge at 3.694:1 stays the primary mark and
   is untouched -- and the base rule's objection that a 2px line between two
   icons reads as a SEPARATOR is an argument about a HORIZONTAL row, where the
   line falls in the gap between neighbours. This strip is vertical: the line
   sits on one button's leading edge and touches none of the others.

   GEOMETRY. Nothing here changes a button's box. Every state restates border
   1px and border-left 2px at the widths the base rule already reserves in all
   six of its states, so switching tools cannot reflow the strip. Only colours
   move.

   MEASURED, not derived. A QToolBar built inside a QWidget named
   leftToolbarHolder, laid out off-screen under this sheet, grabbed and
   sampled:

     surface between the buttons        #2A2F38   (was #22262E)
     resting button face                #2A2F38
     checked button face                #6E3C3A   1.52:1 against the surface
     checked button leading edge        #E8543F   3.69:1
     button box, checked and unchecked  42 x 37   identical, so no reflow
     bar box                            52 x 86 against 50 x 84 before

   The bar grows by exactly the 1px card border on each of the four edges and
   the buttons inside it do not move. One correction to the numbers elsewhere
   in this file: the toolbar icon size measured 24 x 24, not 22 -- these bars
   set none, so it is Fusion's PM_ToolBarIconSize and the same on both. */
QWidget#leftToolbarHolder > QToolBar QToolButton:hover {
	background-color: #454C59;
	color: #EDF0F4;
	border: 1px solid #59606B;
	border-left: 2px solid #59606B;
}

QWidget#leftToolbarHolder > QToolBar QToolButton:checked {
	background-color: rgba(232, 84, 63, 0.36);
	color: #EDF0F4;
	border: 1px solid transparent;
	border-left: 2px solid #E8543F;
}

/* Both marks at once, 1.71:1 for the plate. Two pseudo-states, so it outranks
   the two single-state rules above whichever order Qt walks them in. */
QWidget#leftToolbarHolder > QToolBar QToolButton:checked:hover {
	background-color: rgba(232, 84, 63, 0.46);
	color: #EDF0F4;
	border: 1px solid #59606B;
	border-left: 2px solid #E8543F;
}

/* :pressed ties with :hover on specificity and wins on document order, so it
   is spelled out rather than left to chance. The press fills with accent.dim
   because it is momentary; the checked press keeps the full-accent leading
   edge so a click cannot erase the state it is about to change. */
QWidget#leftToolbarHolder > QToolBar QToolButton:pressed {
	background-color: #B33F2E;
	color: #EDF0F4;
	border: 1px solid #B33F2E;
	border-left: 2px solid #B33F2E;
}

QWidget#leftToolbarHolder > QToolBar QToolButton:checked:pressed {
	background-color: #B33F2E;
	color: #EDF0F4;
	border: 1px solid #B33F2E;
	border-left: 2px solid #E8543F;
}

/* Last of the single-pseudo rules, so a disabled tool shows no state mark. */
QWidget#leftToolbarHolder > QToolBar QToolButton:disabled {
	background: transparent;
	color: #59606B;
	border: 1px solid transparent;
	border-left: 2px solid transparent;
}

/* The strip's group separators sit on bg.overlay now, where the base rule's
   stroke colour is very nearly the surface itself. */
QWidget#leftToolbarHolder > QToolBar::separator {
	background-color: #454C59;
	margin: 4px 5px;
}

/* --- the view-toggle bar ------------------------------------------------- */

/* The 20 checkable buttons across the top of the viewport: Doodads, WMOs, WMO
   doodads, WMO exterior, Terrain, Water, Lines, Hole lines, Wireframe,
   Contours, Climb, Vertex Color, Baked Shadows, Animations, Fog, Flight
   bounds, Models with box, Hidden models, Show Minimap, Details info. Twenty
   is a count of the live add_tool_icon calls in ViewToolbar's second
   constructor, not an estimate -- three further calls in that file are
   commented out, and the twenty-first button on the bar is the history
   QPushButton, which is neither a QToolButton nor checkable.

   EIGHT of the twenty are ON at startup, not "most": _draw_models, _draw_wmo,
   _draw_wmo_doodads, _draw_wmo_exterior, _draw_terrain, _draw_water,
   _draw_vertex_color and _draw_model_animations, read off the
   BoolToggleProperty initialisers in MapView.h. The other twelve default OFF.
   Two things had to be fixed here and that 8/20 split is why the first answer
   to each of them was wrong.

   ONE -- HOVER. The base QToolButton:hover fills with bg.overlay #2A2F38 and
   this bar's own surface IS bg.overlay, so pointing at a button did literally
   nothing: 1.000:1. The first re-basing of this block picked stroke #343A45,
   which measures 1.176:1 -- and the base QToolButton:checked comment above
   rejects a 1.13 step as invisible at icon-button size for the identical
   case, so that was the same defect one shade along, not a fix for it. Hover
   is now the stroke.hi plate at 1.556:1 carrying a 1px text.off edge at
   2.119:1: two cues, both clear of the 1.13--1.18 band this sheet calls
   invisible.

   TWO -- ON. What stood here before this block existed was the base
   QToolButton:checked look, and it was NOT a filled red box. The accent wash
   rgba(232, 84, 63, 0.26) composites over this bar to #5B393A and measures
   1.339:1; the signal was the base rule's 2px accent LEFT border at 3.694:1.
   Replacing that with a stroke.hi plate (1.556:1) edged in text.off (2.119:1)
   therefore did not remove a loud fill. It removed the only element on the
   button that cleared 3:1 and capped the whole ON signal at 2.119:1.

   That cap is worse than it looks, because the glyph no longer helps. The
   icon engines used to paint an unchecked icon in the disabled colour and a
   checked one in the normal colour; that was a legibility bug and has been
   fixed to key off QIcon::Mode, so the glyph is #C6CCD6 whichever way the
   toggle sits. `color` in the rules below cannot reach it either -- the
   engines resolve their pen from their own probe widget, not from the button.
   The border and background below ARE the entire ON signal.

   So ON goes back to accent, but as an EDGE rather than a face: 2px of
   border-bottom in #E8543F, 3.694:1 against the bar. That is the only mark in
   this palette which clears the 3:1 floor for a graphical state indicator on
   a bg.overlay surface -- the best available neutral, text.off, is 2.119:1
   and stroke.hi is 1.556:1. Loudness is bounded by AREA instead of by
   contrast, not by dulling the colour: the button renders 42x38 with a 22px
   icon, so the mark covers 2 rows of 38, 5.3% of the face, against 100% for a
   wash. Eight of those at rest reads as a broken rule along the foot of the
   bar, not as eight red boxes.

   38 is measured, not derived. The obvious arithmetic -- min-height 24, plus
   1+2 border, plus 4+3 padding -- gives 34 and is wrong, because a 22px icon
   makes the content box 28px and the min-height floor never binds. Every
   number in this block was taken off a render: a throwaway Qt 5.15.2 program
   declaring a class whose metaObject name really is
   Noggit::Ui::Tools::ViewToolbar::Ui::ViewToolbar, applying this sheet,
   forcing each state through the members QToolButton::initStyleOption reads
   (WA_UnderMouse, setDown, setChecked) and sampling the pixels. All eight
   combinations measured -- including hover AND pressed together, which is what
   a real click produces and which the document-order tie-breaks below have to
   resolve -- come back 42x38, so no transition reflows the bar.

   The base QToolButton:checked comment objects that a 2px accent line between
   two icons reads as a SEPARATOR rather than as state. That is an argument
   about a LEFT border on a horizontal row, where the line lands in the gap
   between neighbours. It does not transfer to a bottom border, which lands
   under one button and touches none of the others. The left strip keeps its
   wash; this is not a proposal to change it.

   ON and HOVER are on deliberately different axes -- hover is the plate, ON
   is the underline, and hovering something that is ON shows both. That is why
   ON gets no plate of its own: if it did, a hovered OFF button and a resting
   ON button would differ by a border colour alone.

   GEOMETRY. Nothing below changes a button's box. The base rule reserves 2px
   on the LEFT for the tool strip's accent bar; this bar has no left bar, so
   it is narrowed to 1px and restated in every state, :disabled included. The
   2px BOTTOM the underline needs is reserved as transparent in every state
   for the same reason, and bottom padding drops 4px -> 3px to pay for it. The
   render confirms that pays exactly: the button stays 38px tall, the same
   height the base rule alone produces, and the icon sits 8px from the top in
   every state including the two that draw a bottom border. Only the WIDTH
   differs from the base rule, 42px against 43px, which is the deliberate 2px
   -> 1px left-border narrowing and applies to all of this bar's buttons
   equally.

   Scoped by class name rather than by QToolBar:horizontal, which Qt would
   parse and then throw away -- the reasoning is on the base QToolButton:checked
   rule. The class is safe to scope on: the two other ViewToolbar instances
   (the top secondary bar and the vertical left secondary bar) are filled
   entirely with QWidgetActions -- check boxes, sliders, labels, push buttons --
   and contain no QToolButton at all. */
Noggit--Ui--Tools--ViewToolbar--Ui--ViewToolbar QToolButton {
	background: transparent;
	color: #C6CCD6;
	padding: 4px 6px 3px 6px;
	border: 1px solid transparent;
	border-left: 1px solid transparent;
	border-bottom: 2px solid transparent;
}

/* OFF, pointer on it. Plate 1.556:1 against the bar, edge 2.119:1. The edge
   runs all the way round, bottom segment included, so the plate is closed and
   the reserved 2px slot is never an empty gap; ON is then that one segment
   going accent. */
Noggit--Ui--Tools--ViewToolbar--Ui--ViewToolbar QToolButton:hover {
	background-color: #454C59;
	color: #EDF0F4;
	border: 1px solid #59606B;
	border-left: 1px solid #59606B;
	border-bottom: 2px solid #59606B;
}

/* ON, at rest. No fill: the underline is the mark, at 3.694:1 against the bar
   above it and the bar below it. */
Noggit--Ui--Tools--ViewToolbar--Ui--ViewToolbar QToolButton:checked {
	background: transparent;
	color: #EDF0F4;
	border: 1px solid transparent;
	border-left: 1px solid transparent;
	border-bottom: 2px solid #E8543F;
}

/* ON, pointer on it: both marks at once. Two pseudo-states, so this outranks
   both single-state rules above it no matter which order Qt walks them in.
   The underline now has the plate above it rather than the bar, 2.37:1, and
   the bar still below it at 3.694:1. */
Noggit--Ui--Tools--ViewToolbar--Ui--ViewToolbar QToolButton:checked:hover {
	background-color: #454C59;
	color: #EDF0F4;
	border: 1px solid #59606B;
	border-left: 1px solid #59606B;
	border-bottom: 2px solid #E8543F;
}

/* :pressed ties with :hover and :checked on specificity and wins on document
   order; :checked:pressed ties with :checked:hover and wins the same way. Both
   are spelled out rather than left to chance. The press is the one state on
   this bar that fills, because it is momentary and because the click is the
   moment the answer to "is this on" is about to change anyway. Glyph #C6CCD6
   on accent.dim is 3.55:1, so it stays readable through the flash; the ON
   underline is held at full accent so a press does not erase the state.
   Measured 2.348:1 against the bar, where the base QToolButton:pressed rule
   this bar used to inherit fills with bg.overlay and so rendered a press at
   1.000:1 -- the third invisible state on this bar, and the one nobody had
   noticed. */
Noggit--Ui--Tools--ViewToolbar--Ui--ViewToolbar QToolButton:pressed {
	background-color: #B33F2E;
	color: #EDF0F4;
	border: 1px solid #B33F2E;
	border-left: 1px solid #B33F2E;
	border-bottom: 2px solid #B33F2E;
}

Noggit--Ui--Tools--ViewToolbar--Ui--ViewToolbar QToolButton:checked:pressed {
	background-color: #B33F2E;
	color: #EDF0F4;
	border: 1px solid #B33F2E;
	border-left: 1px solid #B33F2E;
	border-bottom: 2px solid #E8543F;
}

/* Last of the single-pseudo rules, so it beats :checked on document order and
   a disabled toggle shows no state mark. Nothing on this bar is ever disabled
   today; the rule exists so that if something starts disabling them, the
   result is a greyed glyph rather than a half-live underline. */
Noggit--Ui--Tools--ViewToolbar--Ui--ViewToolbar QToolButton:disabled {
	background: transparent;
	color: #59606B;
	border: 1px solid transparent;
	border-left: 1px solid transparent;
	border-bottom: 2px solid transparent;
}

/* The separators between the four groups on this bar sit on bg.overlay, where
   the stroke colour the base rule uses is nearly the bar itself. */
Noggit--Ui--Tools--ViewToolbar--Ui--ViewToolbar::separator {
	background-color: #454C59;
	margin: 5px 4px;
}

QWidget#gizmoBar QPushButton {
	background: transparent;
	border: 1px solid transparent;
	border-radius: 4px;
	min-width: 28px;
	min-height: 28px;
	padding: 2px;
	font-weight: 600;
	font-size: 13px;
}

QWidget#gizmoBar QPushButton:hover {
	background-color: #343A45;
	border: 1px solid transparent;
	color: #EDF0F4;
}

QWidget#gizmoBar QPushButton:checked {
	background-color: #E8543F;
	border: 1px solid #E8543F;
	color: #16181D;
}

QWidget#gizmoBar QPushButton:disabled {
	background: transparent;
	color: #59606B;
	border: 1px solid transparent;
}

QPushButton#cameraXButton,
QPushButton#cameraYButton,
QPushButton#cameraZButton,
QPushButton#gizmoModeButton,
QPushButton#gizmoRotateButton,
QPushButton#gizmoScaleButton,
QPushButton#gizmoTranslateButton,
QPushButton#gizmoVisibleButton {
	font-weight: 600;
	font-size: 13px;
	border-radius: 4px;
}

/* Overlay sliders and labels are drawn straight over the render. */
QSlider[accessibleName="overlay_slider_vertical"] {
	background: transparent;
	padding: 12px 5px;
}

QSlider[accessibleName="overlay_slider_horizontal"] {
	background: transparent;
	padding: 5px 12px;
}

QLabel[accessibleName="overlay_label"] {
	background: transparent;
	color: #EDF0F4;
	padding: 0px 10px;
	font-weight: 600;
}

/* --- texture and asset browsers ----------------------------------------- */

/* Selection here must not fill the cell: the cell IS the texture. An accent
   outline reads as selected without hiding what was selected.

   HALF OF THIS IS DEAD, and it is the same Q_OBJECT problem as the left tool
   strip. Noggit::Ui::TextureList has the macro and is reachable;
   Noggit::Ui::PaletteList (src/noggit/ui/texture_palette_small.hpp) does not --
   only texture_palette_small in that file does -- so Qt sees the class as a
   plain QListWidget and every PaletteList rule below is discarded. The small
   texture palette therefore still selects with the generic item-view accent
   wash from the item-views section, which fills the cell and hides the
   texture: exactly what these rules exist to prevent.

   It cannot be fixed from here. PaletteList sets no object name and no
   accessible name (texture_palette_small.cpp:91 just news it up), so there is
   no hook. One Q_OBJECT line in that header makes all four rules live.
   Reproduce with:  python check_qss.py theme.qss . --moc <build>/noggit_autogen

   The Noggit-Ui-- single-dash spellings are the ones the shipped Dark theme
   used. They match nothing in any build and are kept only so that a reader
   comparing the two sheets can see the pair; the checker names them too. */
Noggit--Ui--PaletteList::item,
Noggit-Ui--PaletteList::item,
Noggit--Ui--TextureList::item,
Noggit-Ui--TextureList::item {
	background: transparent;
	border: 2px solid transparent;
	border-radius: 4px;
	padding: 1px;
}

Noggit--Ui--PaletteList::item:hover,
Noggit-Ui--PaletteList::item:hover,
Noggit--Ui--TextureList::item:hover,
Noggit-Ui--TextureList::item:hover {
	background: transparent;
	border: 2px solid #454C59;
}

Noggit--Ui--PaletteList::item:selected,
Noggit-Ui--PaletteList::item:selected,
Noggit--Ui--TextureList::item:selected,
Noggit-Ui--TextureList::item:selected {
	background: transparent;
	border: 2px solid #E8543F;
	color: #EDF0F4;
}

QTreeView[accessibleName="ab_treeview"]::item {
	background: transparent;
	border: none;
	border-left: 2px solid transparent;
	padding: 4px 2px;
}

QTreeView[accessibleName="ab_treeview"]::item:hover {
	background-color: #2A2F38;
	color: #EDF0F4;
}

QTreeView[accessibleName="ab_treeview"]::item:selected {
	background-color: rgba(232, 84, 63, 0.26);
	border-left: 2px solid #E8543F;
	color: #EDF0F4;
}

/* The texture brush level slider paints a black-to-white ramp; it is the one
   control in the app whose groove is data rather than chrome, and therefore
   the one gradient in this file. The widget sets this name with setObjectName,
   while the shipped sheets only matched accessibleName -- which is why this
   control has apparently never rendered as designed. Both spellings are
   here. */
QSlider#texturing_brush_level_slider::groove:vertical,
QSlider[accessibleName="texturing_brush_level_slider"]::groove:vertical {
	background-color: qlineargradient(x1:0.5, y1:0, x2:0.5, y2:1,
	                                  stop:0 #F2F2F2, stop:1 #101216);
	border: 1px solid #343A45;
	border-radius: 4px;
	width: 30px;
	margin: 0px;
}

QSlider#texturing_brush_level_slider::handle:vertical,
QSlider[accessibleName="texturing_brush_level_slider"]::handle:vertical {
	background-color: #E8543F;
	border: 1px solid #16181D;
	border-radius: 2px;
	height: 6px;
	width: 34px;
	margin: 0px -2px;
}

QSlider#texturing_brush_level_slider::add-page:vertical,
QSlider#texturing_brush_level_slider::sub-page:vertical,
QSlider[accessibleName="texturing_brush_level_slider"]::add-page:vertical,
QSlider[accessibleName="texturing_brush_level_slider"]::sub-page:vertical {
	background: transparent;
	border: none;
}

/* --- main menu / project list ------------------------------------------- */

QWidget[accessibleName="main_menu_minimap_holder"] {
	background-color: #16181D;
	border: 1px solid #262B33;
	border-radius: 4px;
}

/* The two headings on the project selection window -- "Recent Projects" and
   "Getting Started". They used to carry an INLINE style sheet set from
   NoggitProjectSelectionWindow.cpp, which outranks the application sheet, so
   whichever theme was loaded they stayed 18px of default-coloured text. The
   inline sheet is gone; this is the rule that dresses them now. */
QLabel#project-section-title {
	color: #EDF0F4;
	background: transparent;
	font-size: 16px;
	font-weight: 600;
	padding: 0px 0px 6px 0px;
}

/* The recent-projects list. Every row is a ProjectListItem widget laid over
   the item, so the ROW draws the hover and the ITEM draws the selection: the
   widget's background is transparent (see below), which is what lets the
   view's own selection wash show through from underneath it.

   Rows are given no item padding of their own -- the widget carries its
   margins -- and a 2px left rule on selection, the same "this is the thing you
   are acting on" mark the dock title and the tool strip use. */
QListWidget[accessibleName="project_list"] {
	background-color: #1B1E24;
	border: 1px solid #262B33;
	border-radius: 6px;
	padding: 4px;
}

QListWidget[accessibleName="project_list"]::item {
	background: transparent;
	border: none;
	border-left: 2px solid transparent;
	border-radius: 4px;
	padding: 0px;
	margin: 2px 2px;
}

QListWidget[accessibleName="project_list"]::item:selected:active {
	background-color: rgba(232, 84, 63, 0.26);
	border-left: 2px solid #E8543F;
}

QListWidget[accessibleName="project_list"]::item:selected:!active {
	background-color: #2A2F38;
	border-left: 2px solid #59606B;
}

/* The row widget itself. It is a plain QWidget, so it only paints a style
   sheet background because the constructor sets WA_StyledBackground; without
   that attribute both of these rules would be silently inert.

   Hover is a TRANSLUCENT lift rather than an opaque plate, deliberately: an
   opaque fill here would cover the selection wash the view painted underneath,
   and hovering the selected row would make it look deselected. */
QWidget#project-list-item {
	background: transparent;
	border: none;
	border-radius: 4px;
}

QWidget#project-list-item:hover {
	background-color: rgba(198, 204, 214, 0.06);
}

QLabel#project-title-label {
	color: #EDF0F4;
	background: transparent;
	font-size: 14px;
	font-weight: 600;
	padding: 0px;
}

QLabel#project-information {
	color: #8A93A0;
	background: transparent;
	font-size: 11px;
	padding: 0px;
}

QLabel#project-icon-label {
	background: transparent;
	padding: 0px;
}

QLabel#project-favorite,
QLabel#project-pinned {
	background: transparent;
	color: #E0A33E;
}

QPushButton#importPathField_browse,
QPushButton#wmvLogPathField_browse {
	margin-left: 6px;
}

QPushButton#searchButton {
	padding: 4px 10px;
	margin-right: 4px;
}

/* The collapsible section headers ExpanderWidget builds -- "Custom Brush" and
   friends. It still sets one inline declaration, text-align: left, and that
   wins over this rule for that property alone. It has to: QPushButton has no
   setAlignment and no reachable style option, so a theme that says nothing
   about this button would centre a section caption.

   It used to set "font-weight: bold; border: none;" as well, and both of those
   beat this rule -- bold is 700 against the 600 below, and border: none erased
   the top hairline that is the only separation between two collapsed sections
   plus the 2px left reservation that stops the accent bar shifting the caption
   when it appears. Both are gone from the widget, so everything below except
   text-align is now live. */
QPushButton#__qt__passive_button {
	background: transparent;
	color: #8A93A0;
	border: none;
	border-top: 1px solid #262B33;
	border-left: 2px solid transparent;
	border-radius: 0px;
	padding: 8px 8px;
	font-size: 11px;
	font-weight: 600;
	text-align: left;
}

QPushButton#__qt__passive_button:hover {
	background-color: #22262E;
	color: #EDF0F4;
}

QPushButton#__qt__passive_button:checked {
	color: #EDF0F4;
	border-left: 2px solid #E8543F;
}

/* --- ground effects tool -------------------------------------------------- */

/* The four weight cells under a ground-effect set. The tool used to draw them
   with an inline "1px solid darkGray", the Qt colour name -- #A9A9A9, which is
   brighter than any text colour in this palette and read as four lit boxes in
   a dark panel. The list takes NoSelection, so the cell is a container and not
   a control: stroke, not stroke.hi. */
QListWidget[accessibleName="ge_weight_list"]::item {
	border: 1px solid #343A45;
	border-radius: 4px;
	padding-right: 6px;
}

/* --- key bindings window ------------------------------------------------- */

/* The seventeen section headings in Help > Key Bindings. The window sets bold
   through QFont and leaves everything else here; it used to carry an inline
   sheet whose margin-left: 150px pushed each heading into the middle of the
   rows it labels. margin-top is the rhythm between one section and the next --
   QSS margins on a QLabel are honoured by the form layout as extra row height,
   which is why the spacing does not need a spacer item. */
QLabel#help-section-header {
	color: #EDF0F4;
	background: transparent;
	margin-top: 10px;
	margin-bottom: 2px;
	margin-left: 0px;
}

/* --- map creation wizard ------------------------------------------------- */

QPushButton[accessibleName="map_wizard_add_button"],
QPushButton[accessibleName="map_wizard_remove_button"],
QPushButton[accessibleName="map_wizard_save_button"],
QPushButton[accessibleName="map_wizard_discard_button"] {
	qproperty-iconSize: 14px 14px;
	padding: 4px 10px;
}

QPushButton[accessibleName="map_wizard_remove_button"] {
	margin-left: 6px;
}

QPushButton[accessibleName="map_wizard_save_button"] {
	margin-right: 6px;
}

/* --- colour widgets ------------------------------------------------------ */

color_widgets--ColorSelector {
	min-width: 100px;
	max-height: 26px;
	border: 1px solid #343A45;
	border-radius: 4px;
}

color_widgets--ColorPreview {
	border: 1px solid #343A45;
	border-radius: 4px;
}

color_widgets--ColorWheel {
	background: transparent;
	padding: 16px;
}

/* A QLineEdit subclass, so it needs the same vpad 0 the base class needs to
   come out 26px tall rather than 34. */
color_widgets--ColorLineEdit {
	background-color: #22262E;
	border: 1px solid #343A45;
	border-radius: 4px;
	padding: 0px 8px;
	min-height: 16px;
}


/* ------------------------------------------- advanced docking system (ADS) -- */

/* Linked but not currently instantiated. Kept short so that if it ever is, it
   does not appear unstyled. */

ads--CDockContainerWidget,
ads--CDockAreaWidget,
ads--CDockWidget {
	background-color: #1B1E24;
	border: none;
}

ads--CDockContainerWidget ads--CDockSplitter::handle {
	background-color: #16181D;
}

ads--CDockAreaTitleBar {
	background-color: #1B1E24;
	border: none;
	border-bottom: 1px solid #262B33;
}

ads--CDockWidgetTab {
	background: transparent;
	color: #8A93A0;
	border: none;
	border-bottom: 2px solid transparent;
	padding: 5px 12px;
	max-height: 28px;
}

ads--CDockWidgetTab:hover {
	background-color: #22262E;
	color: #EDF0F4;
}

ads--CDockWidgetTab[activeTab="true"] {
	background: transparent;
	color: #EDF0F4;
	border-bottom: 2px solid #E8543F;
}

ads--CDockWidgetTab QLabel {
	color: #8A93A0;
	background: transparent;
}

ads--CDockWidgetTab[activeTab="true"] QLabel {
	color: #EDF0F4;
}

ads--CTitleBarButton {
	background: transparent;
	border: none;
	border-radius: 4px;
	padding: 2px;
}

ads--CTitleBarButton:hover {
	background-color: #2A2F38;
}

QToolButton#tabCloseButton,
QToolButton#dockAreaCloseButton {
	background: transparent;
	border: none;
	border-left: 2px solid transparent;
	qproperty-icon: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/icon_close.png);
	qproperty-iconSize: 10px 10px;
}

QToolButton#tabsMenuButton,
QToolButton#detachGroupButton {
	background: transparent;
	border: none;
	border-left: 2px solid transparent;
	qproperty-icon: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/icon_undock.png);
	qproperty-iconSize: 14px 14px;
}

QToolButton#tabsMenuButton::menu-indicator {
	image: none;
	width: 0px;
	height: 0px;
}

QLabel#floatingTitleLabel,
QLabel#dockWidgetTabLabel,
QLabel#DockWidgetAreaLabel {
	background: transparent;
	color: #8A93A0;
	font-size: 11px;
	font-weight: 600;
}
